---
/**
 * hospitality / FooterBK — "Villa Marindu"'s closing rail.
 *
 * ROLE. The design's last band, reproduced: the darkest ground on the page
 * (--night-3) under one hairline, a 1.6fr / 1fr / 1fr grid at 40px gap — the
 * wordmark lock with its blurb and one quiet brass action, a page-links column,
 * a contact column — closed by a hairline and then the two-part colophon row in
 * 10px tracked caps. It is the last place a guest looks for the phone and the
 * address before they give up, so every column is real owner data or absent.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — every visible string here, and nothing else. All of them are
 * declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts, so each is a hook a
 * sidebar input actually owns:
 *   footer.brand                 — the Prata wordmark (also HeaderBK's; one
 *                                  field, two places, on purpose)
 *   footer.locality              — the brass locality tag on its baseline
 *   footer.blurb                 — the standing line under the lock
 *   footer.explore.title         — the PAGE-LINKS column heading (see decision 1)
 *   footer.visit.title           — the CONTACT column heading (see decision 1)
 *   navbar_links.${i}.label      — one page-column link's label
 *   navbar_links.${i}.href       — that link's target (data-href-field)
 *   navCtaText                   — the contact action's label (drawn twice, as
 *   navCtaHref                     the design draws it — see decision 4)
 *   contact.email                        — the mailto row. NOT in the design,
 *                                          whose demo villa took enquiries on
 *                                          Messenger — but a business that has
 *                                          an email had it appear nowhere.
 *   location.phone               — the tel: row (data-field only, decision 7)
 *   location.address             — the address row, and the colophon's identity
 *   footer.notes.${i}            — the colophon's right-hand note rows
 *
 * DERIVED (computed here, never authored here):
 *   · the wordmark falls back to layout.businessName when content.footer.brand
 *     is blank — the same name out of the same submission, not an invention.
 *   · the blurb falls back to footer.brand_blurb, the schema's own alias.
 *   · phone and address fall back to layout.contact.* exactly as LocationBK
 *     resolves them, so the two sections can never print different numbers.
 *   · the tel: href is built from the phone's own digits.
 *   · every in-page href is resolved by onPage() against the ids PageBK really
 *     renders (see the anchor guard below).
 *   · `i` on a page link is the ORIGINAL, pre-filter array index (R6), so
 *     dropping an unreachable row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3.
 *   · the colophon's address is FLATTENED to one line (newlines to commas) to
 *     fit a single row of tracked caps, which is why that node — and only that
 *     node — carries data-derived: editorBridge's live ed:update would other-
 *     wise paint the raw multi-line scalar into a caps row. The column's own
 *     address is NOT derived: it is the stored string verbatim, broken by
 *     `white-space: pre-line`, so an inline edit stays truthful.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   · THE DESIGN'S OWN COPY. "Villa Marindu", "Palawan", "This page", "Get in
 *     touch", "The villa", "What is included", "Gallery", "FAQ", the blurb and
 *     the address are the DESIGNER filling in their own template. Not one of
 *     those words is in this file.
 *   · "RATES IN PHILIPPINE PESOS" — the design's right-hand colophon cell. A
 *     currency statement is a claim about how this business quotes, and no
 *     declared field carries it. The cell is fed by footer.notes instead: rows
 *     the owner typed (lib/derive-content-defaults.ts seeds the © line there
 *     and deliberately suppresses a bare '© 2026' with no name, so there is
 *     nothing to invent). No rows, no cell.
 *   · a column heading with no column under it, a wordmark with no name, a link
 *     with no label or no reachable target, a colophon row with no notes, and
 *     the whole grid when all three columns are empty. Every guard sits OUTSIDE
 *     its tag, so a blank value removes the ELEMENT, not just its text.
 *   · no social chips: the design draws none, and FooterBJ already gives
 *     footer.social a surface in this family.
 *   · no floating message pill: this design has no FAB.
 *   · no .reveal. The footer is the page terminus; fading in the last thing on
 *     screen buys nothing and risks a footer that never un-hides.
 *
 * DECISIONS a reader would otherwise take for an oversight
 *
 * 1. WHICH HEADING FIELD FEEDS WHICH COLUMN. The task brief pairs
 *    footer.visit.title with "This page" and footer.explore.title with "Get in
 *    touch". The schema declares the opposite and says so in its own labels:
 *    footer.explore.title is labelled "Links column heading", placeholder
 *    "This page"; footer.visit.title is labelled "Contact column heading",
 *    placeholder "Get in touch" — the design's two words exactly. FooterBJ
 *    binds them that way too. Honouring the brief's pairing would put the
 *    sidebar box named "Contact column heading" in charge of the page-links
 *    column on this one template. The schema's own naming wins; flag if wrong.
 *
 * 2. NO nav LANDMARK AND NO aria-label ON THE LINK COLUMN. Naming it costs a
 *    word this file never received ("Footer", "Secondary"). The links are
 *    ordinary links in a footer, already announced as such (R9).
 *
 * 3. THE ARROW after the brand column's action is CSS generated content, not
 *    markup. editorBridge writes an inline edit back through innerHTML, so a
 *    glyph inside the element could end up stored in navCtaText; a pseudo-
 *    element cannot.
 *
 * 4. THE CONTACT ACTION IS DRAWN TWICE — brand column and contact column —
 *    because the design draws it twice. Both nodes hook the SAME pair
 *    (navCtaText / navCtaHref), so they cannot disagree, and ed:update
 *    refreshes both. It hooks the Header group's pair rather than hero.cta1 on
 *    purpose: components/editor/editorBridge.ts resolves a Blocks toggle by
 *    counting hooks of that prefix inside closest('section,footer'), so a
 *    footer carrying hero.* hooks becomes a candidate for "hide the Hero
 *    block". navCtaText has no entry in that PREFIX map at all, so it is
 *    toggle-inert. HeaderBK hooks the same pair for the same reason.
 *
 * 5. THE TWO location.* HOOKS HERE ARE SAFE for the same weighted resolution:
 *    this footer holds 2 (phone, address — 3 nodes), LocationBK holds its tag,
 *    headline, sub, address, phone, hours and rules, so "hide Location" still
 *    hides the Location section. Recorded, not fixed; it is a shared-file
 *    concern. The footer's own toggle resolves here easily (5 footer.* hooks
 *    against HeaderBK's 2).
 *
 * 6. NO .btn IN THIS FILE, AT ALL. lib/genericThemeOverrides.ts emits
 *    `footer .btn { color: <on-ink> !important }` for footers sitting on an ink
 *    fill; a .btn here would take that pinned label colour whatever the design
 *    says. The actions are .linkc and plain links, which it never touches.
 *    None of the forbidden names (.nav-cta, .btn-primary, .testi, .cta-band,
 *    .trust) appear either.
 *
 * 7. THE tel: ANCHOR CARRIES data-field ONLY. Its href is derived from the
 *    field's own value, so a data-href-field there would write "tel:+63…" back
 *    into the phone box.
 *
 * 8. THE COLOPHON SEPARATOR is an aria-hidden span with no data-field, drawn
 *    only when both halves exist, so it can never be edited into the content
 *    and is never announced.
 *
 * CONTRAST, the design's palette on this band's ground. --night-3 is DARKER
 * than --night, so every ratio PageBK verified holds or improves here:
 * bone-hi 17+, grey 8.57, accent ~6.9. The two the design accepts below AA are
 * the same two the wrapper records: --muted at 14px (blurb, 4.48:1) and --faint
 * in the colophon at 10px caps (3.16:1). Both are the owner's palette and the
 * owner's call; the file ships the design's values rather than re-deriving them.
 *
 * Visibility: PageBK gates this file on visibility.footerSection. Colour:
 * wrapper tokens only, no literal hex anywhere (R1).
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;
const c = (content as any) || {};
const lay = (layout as any) || {};
const f = c.footer || {};

// ── ANCHOR GUARD — byte-identical to HeaderBK's, so the rail and the footer can
// never send a guest to different places. lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-
// codes nav links to '#about', '#services', '#why', '#work', '#visit' and
// navCtaHref to '#visit'; lib/astro-builder.ts falls back to '#about',
// '#services', '#gallery', '#contact'. Most of those ids no design renders.
// Resolve every in-page href against what PageBK ACTUALLY renders: keep it if
// the id exists, remap it when this design carries the same material under
// another name, otherwise fall back — and a links column passes '' as the
// fallback, because a dead entry in a wayfinding list is worse than a missing
// one. Off-page hrefs (tel:, mailto:, http) pass through untouched.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['about', 'services', 'gallery', 'location', 'faq', 'main']);
// '#services' is what is INCLUDED in the let (rooms and amenities land there),
// '#about' is the villa itself (host and why are one block here), '#work' is the
// photo mosaic, '#visit' / '#contact' both mean the location block, and a
// booking link has no checkout to reach on this page, so it lands on <main>.
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  amenities: 'services',
  book: 'main',
  contact: 'location',
  host: 'about',
  rooms: 'services',
  visit: 'location',
  why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const id = href.slice(1);
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(id)) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[id];
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

// The wordmark. 'Your Business' once shipped as a real customer's own mark —
// with nothing supplied the lock is omitted instead of inventing a name.
const brand = String(f.brand ?? lay.businessName ?? '').trim();
// One field, three consumers (the rail, this lock, the map caption). Nothing
// derives it, so on a first build it is simply absent rather than guessed out
// of the address.
const locality = String(f.locality ?? '').trim();
// footer.brand_blurb is the schema's declared alias for the same box.
const blurb = String(f.blurb ?? f.brand_blurb ?? '').trim();

// The page's one action, from the Header group (see decision 4). '#location' is
// the fallback because that is where this design puts the address, the map and
// the house rules; there is no checkout to send a booking link to.
const ctaText = String(c.navCtaText ?? '').trim();
const ctaHref = onPage(c.navCtaHref, '#location');
// Computed, never literal, and ONE conditional per attribute — an owner may
// point this at #location or a tel:, and forcing those into a new tab is wrong.
const ctaExternal = /^https?:/i.test(ctaHref);

// Column headings. Bound, not hardcoded, and guarded — an owner who clears the
// box gets a column with no heading, not the word the designer happened to use.
const pageTitle = String(f.explore?.title ?? '').trim();
const contactTitle = String(f.visit?.title ?? '').trim();

// The page column reads layout.navLinks — the same array HeaderBK reads, which
// lib/astro-builder.ts maps from content.navbar_links — while hooking
// `navbar_links.${i}.*`, the path the sidebar owns. Reading content directly
// here would let the rail and the footer disagree after a builder fallback.
const pageLinks: Array<{ label: string; href: string; i: number }> =
  (Array.isArray(lay.navLinks) ? lay.navLinks : [])
    .map((l: any, i: number) => ({ label: String(l?.label ?? '').trim(), href: onPage(l?.href, ''), i }))
    .filter((l: { label: string; href: string }) => l.label && l.href);

// Contact rows. Same resolution order LocationBK uses, so one page cannot print
// two different numbers or two different addresses.
const phone = String(c.location?.phone ?? lay.contact?.phone ?? '').trim();
// The design draws no email row — its demo villa took enquiries on Messenger —
// but contact.email is schema-declared, FooterBJ prints it, and a real business
// that supplies one had it appear NOWHERE on the page. Same resolution order as
// the phone above.
const email = String((c as any).contact?.email ?? lay.contact?.email ?? '').trim();
const phoneDigits = phone.replace(/[^0-9+]/g, '');
const address = String(c.location?.address ?? lay.contact?.address ?? '').trim();
// Colophon identity line: the design prints one line, and location.address is a
// multi-line textarea. Flattened for THIS row only (hence data-derived on it).
const addressFlat = address.replace(/\s*\n+\s*/g, ', ');

// Colophon notes — the owner's own rows, dropped when blank, ORIGINAL index
// carried so an edit lands on the row that was clicked (R6).
const notes: Array<{ v: string; i: number }> = (Array.isArray(f.notes) ? f.notes : [])
  .map((n: any, i: number) => ({ v: String(n ?? '').trim(), i }))
  .filter((n: { v: string }) => n.v);

// GATES — BODY content only. A heading never counts toward its own column, and
// the section never counts a tag or a headline, because lib/derive-content-
// defaults.ts fills those on every site.
const hasBrandCol = Boolean(brand || locality || blurb || ctaText);
// A LABEL PRINTED TWICE IN ONE FOOTER IS A STUTTER, not a design.
//
// The design prints its contact label in BOTH the brand column and this one,
// and that reads correctly there because the two say different things: the
// designer set contactLabel to "Contact us" and headed this column "Get in
// touch". On a real site both come from derive-content-defaults, which seeds
// navCtaText to "Get in touch" — so ONE field printed the same three words
// twice, six lines apart, and an owner asked why their footer said it twice.
//
// The rule: this column shows the action only when the brand column is NOT
// already showing it, and never when it merely repeats this column's heading.
// Nothing is lost — the brand column keeps its arrow link and the rail keeps
// its pill, and what remains here (phone, email, address) are distinct
// channels. Compared case- and whitespace-insensitively, because "Get In Touch"
// is the same stutter.
const norm = (v: string): string => v.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim().toLowerCase();
const brandShowsCta = hasBrandCol && Boolean(ctaText);
const showContactCta =
  Boolean(ctaText) && !brandShowsCta && norm(ctaText) !== norm(contactTitle);
const hasContactCol = Boolean(showContactCta || contactTitle || phone || email || address);
const hasGrid = hasBrandCol || pageLinks.length > 0 || hasContactCol;
const hasColophon = Boolean(brand || addressFlat || notes.length > 0);
---
{(hasGrid || hasColophon) && (
<footer class="foot">
  <div class="wrap">
    {hasGrid && (
      <div class="foot-grid">
        {hasBrandCol && (
          <div class="foot-brand">
            {(brand || locality) && (
              <div class="foot-lock">
                {brand && <span class="foot-wm" data-field="footer.brand">{brand}</span>}
                {locality && <span class="foot-loc" data-field="footer.locality">{locality}</span>}
              </div>
            )}
            {blurb && <p class="foot-blurb" data-field="footer.blurb">{blurb}</p>}
            {/* Both hooks sit on the ANCHOR: the editor's link popover reads
                data-field from whatever carries data-href-field, so a label on
                an inner span leaves it editing an empty path. The arrow is
                generated content (see the stylesheet). */}
            {ctaText && (
              <a
                class="linkc foot-cta"
                href={ctaHref}
                target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
                rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
                data-field="navCtaText"
                data-href-field="navCtaHref"
              >{ctaText}</a>
            )}
          </div>
        )}

        {pageLinks.length > 0 && (
          <div class="foot-col">
            {pageTitle && <h4 class="foot-h" data-field="footer.explore.title">{pageTitle}</h4>}
            {pageLinks.map((l) => (
              <a
                class="foot-link"
                href={l.href}
                data-field={`navbar_links.${l.i}.label`}
                data-href-field={`navbar_links.${l.i}.href`}
              >{l.label}</a>
            ))}
          </div>
        )}

        {hasContactCol && (
          <div class="foot-col">
            {contactTitle && <h4 class="foot-h" data-field="footer.visit.title">{contactTitle}</h4>}
            {showContactCta && (
              <a
                class="foot-link"
                href={ctaHref}
                target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
                rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
                data-field="navCtaText"
                data-href-field="navCtaHref"
              >{ctaText}</a>
            )}
            {(phone || email || address) && (
              <address class="foot-addr">
                {phone && (
                  <a class="foot-link foot-tel" href={`tel:${phoneDigits}`} data-field="location.phone">{phone}</a>
                )}
                {/* mailto: is BUILT, never stored — the visible text stays the
                    plain address so an inline edit can never write "mailto:…"
                    back into the email box. */}
                {email && (
                  <a class="foot-link foot-mail" href={`mailto:${email}`} data-field="contact.email">{email}</a>
                )}
                {/* The stored string verbatim — `white-space: pre-line` draws
                    the owner's own line breaks, so the DOM text and the field
                    value stay identical and a live edit cannot lie. */}
                {address && <span class="foot-addr-l" data-field="location.address">{address}</span>}
              </address>
            )}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    )}

    {hasColophon && (
      <div class="foot-colo">
        {(brand || addressFlat) && (
          <span class="foot-id">
            {brand && <span data-field="footer.brand">{brand}</span>}
            {brand && addressFlat && <span class="foot-sep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>}
            {addressFlat && <span data-field="location.address" data-derived>{addressFlat}</span>}
          </span>
        )}
        {notes.length > 0 && (
          <span class="foot-notes">
            {notes.map((n) => (<span data-field={`footer.notes.${n.i}`}>{n.v}</span>))}
          </span>
        )}
      </div>
    )}
  </div>
</footer>
)}
<style>
  /* The design's closing band: the page's darkest ground under one hairline.
     62px above the grid and 40px below the colophon, exactly as drawn; the top
     figure is a clamp so a 360px screen does not open with 62px of nothing,
     and it tops out at the design's own value. */
  .foot {
    background-color: var(--night-3);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding: clamp(42px, 6vw, 62px) 0 40px;
  }

  /* 1.6fr / 1fr / 1fr at 40px, closed by the hairline the design draws under
     it. minmax(0,…) on every track so a long unbroken address or link label
     cannot widen a column past its share and push the page sideways. */
  .foot-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.6fr) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 40px;
    padding-bottom: 40px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  /* Wordmark and locality tag share a baseline — the same lock as the rail,
     one size smaller and in the brighter bone the design specifies here. */
  .foot-lock {
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 11px; flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-bottom: 15px; min-width: 0;
  }
  .foot-wm {
    font-family: var(--disp); font-weight: 400; font-size: 24px;
    line-height: 1.1; color: var(--bone-hi);
    min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .foot-loc {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 600; font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: .34em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent);
    min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* Qualified by the parent class on purpose: the wrapper's paragraph reset is
     `html[data-page="BK"] :where(p)`, which a bare element rule here would only
     tie with. */
  .foot-brand .foot-blurb {
    margin: 0 0 18px; font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--muted); max-width: 38ch; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* The design's 13px brass action. .linkc already carries the size, the weight
     and the colour, so only the arrow is added — as generated content, so the
     glyph can never be written back into navCtaText through an inline edit.
     It carries NO margin: .linkc is an inline-flex box, so the pseudo-element
     is a flex ITEM and the primitive's own `gap: .5em` already spaces it — a
     margin here stacks on top of that and opens twice the design's gap. */
  .foot-cta { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .foot-cta::after { content: '→'; }

  /* Column headings: 10px tracked caps in brass, the design's own treatment for
     every eyebrow on the page. */
  .foot-h {
    margin: 0 0 15px; font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 600; font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: .3em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent);
    line-height: 1.4; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* The design's rows are 14px grey on a 28px pitch — its <p> carries a 9px
     bottom margin over a default line box, and the LAST row carries none, so
     the column opens no gap above the closing hairline. Blocks rather than
     inline text so the whole row is the tap target: 3px of padding either side
     of a 1.6 line box lands on 28.4px, the design's rhythm, with no margin to
     leave dangling under the last row.
     This design's links change COLOUR on hover and draw NO rule — its own
     global a:hover is --accent-light and nothing else — so there is no border
     here and nothing but colour to transition. */
  .foot-link {
    display: block; padding: 3px 0;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--grey);
    transition: color .18s ease;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* The design's stated hover is --accent-light, which the theme engine maps to
     the scheme's LIGHT accent — so unlike a bare --accent it cannot go dark on
     this ground mid-interaction. */
  .foot-link:hover { color: var(--accent-light); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .foot-link { transition: none; } }

  .foot-addr {
    font-style: normal; margin: 0; min-width: 0;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  /* The address is long and the email is not: the mail row keeps the link
     underline the tel row has, and breaks anywhere rather than widening the
     column at 360px. */
  .foot-mail { display: block; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  /* The address is the stored string, unaltered — pre-line draws the owner's
     own line breaks without this file generating a single <br>. */
  .foot-addr-l {
    display: block; padding: 3px 0;
    white-space: pre-line; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* A tel: row sits inside the address block and otherwise reads exactly like
     the static line beside it — same token, size and weight — so nothing marks
     it as a link until hover (WCAG 1.4.1). Underlined at rest; text-decoration
     adds no box, so thickening it on hover cannot shift the rows. Deliberately
     NOT applied to the page column: those are wayfinding links in a list,
     already unambiguous by position and heading. */
  .foot-addr .foot-tel {
    color: var(--grey);
    text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-color: currentColor;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px; text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
  .foot-addr .foot-tel:hover {
    color: var(--accent-light);
    text-decoration-color: var(--accent-light); text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
  }

  /* The colophon: 24px under the hairline, the two cells pushed apart, 10px
     caps at .24em in the faintest token. Wraps rather than overflows. */
  .foot-colo {
    padding-top: 24px;
    display: flex; justify-content: space-between; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 500; font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: .24em; text-transform: uppercase; line-height: 1.9;
    color: var(--faint);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .foot-id, .foot-notes { min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .foot-notes { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 4px 16px; }
  .foot-sep { margin: 0 .45em; }

  /* ── RESPONSIVE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The wrapper owns the shared 760 / 520 pass; this file owns its own grid.
     Three columns cannot hold their type sizes below ~900px, so they stack
     there — one width, so there is never a band where a column is 90px wide. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .foot-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 30px; padding-bottom: 30px; }
    /* Stacked, each row is a primary target rather than one of three columns. */
    .foot-link { min-height: 44px; display: flex; align-items: center; margin-bottom: 2px; }
    .foot-addr-l { padding: 10px 0; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .foot { padding-bottom: 32px; }
    .foot-wm { font-size: 21px; }
    .foot-loc { letter-spacing: .24em; }
    .foot-brand .foot-blurb { max-width: none; }
    /* Wide tracking is what actually pushes a 320px screen sideways here. */
    .foot-colo { letter-spacing: .14em; gap: 10px; line-height: 1.8; }
    .foot-notes { gap: 4px 10px; }
  }
</style>
